WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.849
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.149 --> 00:00:09.338
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.638 --> 00:00:16.795
Unit nineteen: Digital & Multimedia Texts. Today you will describe digital features with compound adjectives.

4
00:00:17.095 --> 00:00:19.887
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:23.857
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:25.973
Hypertext.

7
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:31.282
Hypertext means text containing links that lead readers to other texts.

8
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:34.591
Hypertext lets readers jump between ideas.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:37.685
Your turn. Say: Hypertext.

10
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:41.604
Hypertext.

11
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.874
Multimedia element.

12
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:49.391
Multimedia element means a picture, sound or video that is part of a text.

13
00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:52.459
Each multimedia element supports the story.

14
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.132
Your turn. Say: Multimedia element.

15
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:00.583
Multimedia element.

16
00:01:01.317 --> 00:01:02.782
Interactive text.

17
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:08.511
Interactive text means writing that readers can click, change or respond to.

18
00:01:09.117 --> 00:01:11.569
An interactive text can change its ending.

19
00:01:12.183 --> 00:01:14.987
Your turn. Say: Interactive text.

20
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:19.299
Interactive text.

21
00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:21.423
Digital publishing.

22
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:26.634
Digital publishing means producing and sharing content through online platforms.

23
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.679
Digital publishing reaches readers instantly.

24
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:32.947
Your turn. Say: Digital publishing.

25
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.115
Digital publishing.

26
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.153
Nonlinear reading.

27
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:45.249
Nonlinear reading means reading in an order chosen by the reader, not from start to end.

28
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.964
Nonlinear reading suits reference websites.

29
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.237
Your turn. Say: Nonlinear reading.

30
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.496
Nonlinear reading.

31
00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.576
Embedded media.

32
00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:03.916
Embedded media means audio or video placed directly inside a page.

33
00:02:04.517 --> 00:02:07.155
Embedded media makes the report more vivid.

34
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.890
Your turn. Say: Embedded media.

35
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:15.245
Embedded media.

36
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:17.391
Digital annotation.

37
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:22.531
Digital annotation means adding notes or highlights to an online text.

38
00:02:23.117 --> 00:02:26.103
Digital annotation helps students share ideas.

39
00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:30.182
Your turn. Say: Digital annotation.

40
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:34.476
Digital annotation.

41
00:02:35.217 --> 00:02:36.647
Reader engagement.

42
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.308
Reader engagement means how actively readers take part in or respond to a text.

43
00:02:42.917 --> 00:02:45.473
Reader engagement rose after the quiz was added.

44
00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:48.887
Your turn. Say: Reader engagement.

45
00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:53.164
Reader engagement.

46
00:02:53.967 --> 00:02:57.501
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:04.880
Visal, a review praises 'a user-friendly interface' and 'a ten-minute video'. What kind of adjectives are these?

48
00:03:05.983 --> 00:03:21.038
Compound adjectives. Two or more words joined by a hyphen before a noun act as one adjective: user-friendly, well-designed, long-term. They make descriptions compact.

49
00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.418
What about numbers?

50
00:03:24.517 --> 00:03:41.440
A number inside a compound adjective stays singular: a ten-minute video, not a ten-minutes video. Compare: The video is ten minutes long. After the noun, we usually do not use a hyphen.

51
00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:44.259
Do we always need a hyphen?

52
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:57.328
Before a noun, yes, if it avoids confusion: a well-known author. But the author is well known. Adverbs ending in -ly do not take a hyphen: a highly rated app.

53
00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:00.083
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:00.917 --> 00:04:07.142
Grammar focus: Compound adjectives: noun and adverb plus participle plus hyphen.

55
00:04:07.442 --> 00:04:08.699
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:09.383 --> 00:04:11.882
The site has a user-friendly interface.

57
00:04:12.783 --> 00:04:16.397
Readers enjoy a ten-minute video essay.

58
00:04:17.283 --> 00:04:19.643
It is a well-designed interactive text.

59
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:30.558
Before a noun, join the words of a compound adjective with a hyphen. A number inside stays singular (a ten-minute video). No hyphen after -ly adverbs.

60
00:04:31.317 --> 00:04:35.211
Be careful! Don't say: It is a ten-minutes video.

61
00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:39.665
Say: It is a ten-minute video.

62
00:04:39.965 --> 00:04:42.882
Keep the number singular in a compound adjective.

63
00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:46.226
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:48.394
Reading on Screen.

65
00:04:48.694 --> 00:04:53.573
Hypertext turns a page into a network of links that readers can follow in any order.

66
00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:59.921
A well-designed interactive text uses embedded media to explain difficult ideas.

67
00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:07.867
Nonlinear reading can be exciting, but a ten-minute video may still hold attention better than a long block of text.

68
00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:15.081
Digital publishing has made research widely available, and digital annotation helps readers share thoughts.

69
00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:21.305
Strong reader engagement depends on a user-friendly design and a meaningful multimedia element.

70
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.309
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:28.627
It is a blank video.

72
00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:33.683
A, five-minute. B, five-minutes. C, five minute.

73
00:05:37.233 --> 00:05:39.976
The answer is A: five-minute.

74
00:05:41.117 --> 00:05:43.836
Text that contains links to other texts is blank.

75
00:05:44.136 --> 00:05:48.033
A, hypertext. B, appendix. C, stanza.

76
00:05:51.567 --> 00:05:53.798
The answer is A: hypertext.

77
00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:56.532
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:56.832 --> 00:06:03.388
A, She wrote a highly-rated review.. B, She wrote a highly rated review.. C, She wrote a high rated-review..

79
00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:09.804
The answer is B: She wrote a highly rated review..

80
00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.902
Reading in an order chosen by the reader is blank reading.

81
00:06:14.202 --> 00:06:17.901
A, linear. B, nonlinear. C, formal.

82
00:06:21.467 --> 00:06:23.826
The answer is B: nonlinear.

83
00:06:24.983 --> 00:06:28.376
According to the reading, what helps readers share thoughts?

84
00:06:28.676 --> 00:06:33.560
A, digital annotation. B, a longer title. C, printed footnotes.

85
00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.750
The answer is A: digital annotation.

86
00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:43.980
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:44.817 --> 00:06:50.380
How does reading a nonlinear, interactive digital text change the reading experience compared to a book?

88
00:06:59.183 --> 00:07:00.591
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:00.891 --> 00:07:27.010
Reading a nonlinear, interactive text gives the reader more control. Instead of following one fixed order, I can click links, watch embedded media and choose which ideas to explore first. This can increase reader engagement, but it may also make it harder to follow one clear argument, whereas a book guides me from the first page to the last.

90
00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.007
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:29.917 --> 00:07:37.538
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and compound adjectives: noun and adverb plus participle plus hyphen.

92
00:07:37.838 --> 00:07:42.487
Today's value is future readiness. Build the skills that tomorrow will ask of you.

93
00:07:42.787 --> 00:07:45.149
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:45.449 --> 00:07:49.054
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

